      "mother": {
        "preparation": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "preparation",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Develop a low-overhead sequential DP candidate. Use one contiguous O(N) DP array: blocked cells reset dp[j] to zero; other cells combine the previous-row dp[j] with the current-row left value. Replace modulo with conditional subtraction, since both operands are below MOD and their sum fits signed int. Eliminate row copying. Compare compact bitmap storage against sorted, deduplicated row-wise obstacle lists without requiring an N×N allocation. Investigate buffered input. Preserve initialization, including N=1 and blocked endpoints. Measure changes separately; compiler optimization may already reduce constant-modulus costs.",
            "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle combinatorial algorithm. Sort and deduplicate blocked coordinates. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2N−2 modulo 1000000007. For each blocked point, count unrestricted paths to it and subtract paths whose first blocked point is an earlier coordinate-wise reachable obstacle. Apply the same subtraction to the destination. This costs O(N+B²) time for B distinct obstacles and avoids visiting every grid cell. Handle blocked endpoints directly and use the binomial formula for obstacle-free inputs. Provide a general DP fallback when obstacle count makes the quadratic method unattractive; choose dispatch from algorithmic costs and permitted measurements, not test identities.",
            "C": "Research four-core parallel DP using cache-sized tiles scheduled in anti-diagonal wavefronts. Each tile must wait for its top and left boundaries; compute its interior with the exact modular recurrence and obstacle resets. Explore frontier/boundary buffers rather than a full integer DP matrix, with compact or sparse obstacle storage. Use persistent workers and coarse tasks to limit synchronization overhead. Sweep a small set of tile sizes and retain a sequential path for small workloads. Use only threading facilities supported by the unchanged build configuration. Demonstrate whether parallelism actually beats the optimized sequential candidate on this short-running workload.",
            "G": "Integrate only correct, measured programs produced by A/B/C. First compare standalone candidates under the unchanged harness, then test combinations individually: shared input/obstacle handling, sparse-combinatorial versus DP dispatch, and sequential versus parallel DP dispatch. Deduplicate obstacles consistently and preserve exact output and modular arithmetic. Verify edge cases through existing permitted correctness checks and mathematical review without changing tests or oracle. Re-measure every integrated candidate; gains are not additive. Retain the fastest validated complete program, including a standalone tree result if integration regresses. Do not change compiler, budgets, target, permissions, tests, or oracle.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source solves Problem D, not the other problems in the statement. Its work is O(N²), with an N×N bit-packed obstacle grid, modular DP updates, and a row-copy pass. The only measured baseline is correct on four cases and averages 0.08109 seconds across three close samples; dimensions and bottlenecks are unknown. ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU favors three complementary investigations: lower sequential overhead, sparse-obstacle algorithmic acceleration, and coarse-grained CPU parallelism. All candidates must remain correct for every valid input; dispatch must depend on actual input structure rather than benchmark identities."
          },
