        "synthesis": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "synthesis",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Refine the scalar/register-blocked kernel from 3927d57c5dfc416a. Compare two-, four-, and eight-row blocking, retaining contiguous sorted/deduplicated obstacles, row offsets, obstacle-free spans, and conditional modular subtraction. Measure preprocessing together with computation; additional registers or obstacle-stream merging may erase savings. Produce a reusable tile kernel accepting incoming top and left boundaries and returning bottom and right boundaries, including partial row groups. Preserve a low-overhead sequential implementation for G.",
            "B": "Refine ARM64 NEON interval scans from d898a646dd394766. Compare existing ordered vector carries against a prefix network over local vector totals, and assess unrolling versus register pressure. Use contiguous obstacle storage and unsynchronized input as separately measured changes. Generalize scan_interval to accept an initial carry: tile boundaries can supply a nonzero carry, whereas obstacles reset it to zero. Reduce every intermediate modular addition safely and preserve scalar tails. Deliver SIMD as an alternative kernel, not an assumed improvement over A's faster register blocking.",
            "C": "Investigate scheduling using e8a1a114d5e24701 and d1a09632dced491f as controls. Compare dependency tasks and static anti-diagonal execution with identical kernels and tile dimensions, then explore tile size/aspect ratio and sequential crossover on the fixed four-core target. Confirm whether the unchanged build actually enables OpenMP before attributing gains to parallelism. Preserve one persistent team, exclusive boundary ownership, and complete dependency ordering. Account for task-generation overhead, wavefront startup/drain, false sharing, and quadratic obstacle-bitmap memory. Do not change compiler settings or execution resources.",
            "G": "Keep all admissible programs as controls; begin with e8a1a114d5e24701, but retain A's 3927d57c5dfc416a as a near-tied sequential alternative. Integrate incrementally: first input handling, then sparse obstacle storage, then A's boundary-aware register-blocked kernel within C's scheduler. Evaluate B's SIMD kernel separately before considering combinations. Sparse tile processing must locate only obstacles inside each tile; initialize every row's left carry from its incoming boundary, not automatically to zero. Measure each integrated program end to end with the unchanged harness and retain the fastest validated complete program—even if it is an unmodified parent. Use only algorithmic dispatch based on dimensions, obstacle structure, and available execution capability, never test identities. Preserve duplicate-obstacle semantics, blocked endpoints, n=1, partial tiles, modular bounds, and sequential/non-OpenMP correctness. Change only permitted solution source; do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
            "analysis": "The active task is Problem D, grid-path counting modulo 1000000007, not the other problems in the statement. Baseline time is 81.09 ms. Task-wavefront C, static-wavefront C, and register-blocked A achieve 13.96, 14.10, and 14.18 ms respectively: roughly 5.7–5.8x baseline, but their approximately 1–2% separation is inconclusive without variability measurements. A's four-row version substantially improves its earlier 23.22 ms scalar scan; B's unrolled NEON improves 20.20 to 18.05 ms but remains slower than the leaders. These are whole-program comparisons, not isolated causal measurements. Prioritize register blocking and controlled scheduling/kernel integration, with SIMD as a competing option. All current candidates still perform quadratic grid work; sparse obstacle storage reduces memory and checks, not that asymptotic cost. Correctness requires exact obstacle resets and boundary propagation; reduced operands permit one subtraction because their sum is at most 2000000012."
          },
